1
00:00:01,000 --> 00:00:26,000
SPEAKERA: Imagine we fire tiny particles, one at a time, at a wall with two narrow slits in it.

2
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:56,000
SPEAKERB: And each one that gets through lands on the screen behind as a single dot.

3
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:26,000
SPEAKERA: At first, the dots seem to land almost anywhere.

4
00:01:31,000 --> 00:01:56,000
SPEAKERB: Just random, then?

5
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:26,000
SPEAKERA: Keep going, though, and they sort themselves into stripes.

6
00:02:31,000 --> 00:02:56,000
SPEAKERB: Stripes like these are what waves make when they overlap.

7
00:03:01,000 --> 00:03:26,000
SPEAKERA: But we sent particles.

8
00:03:31,000 --> 00:03:56,000
SPEAKERB: So what actually went through the slits? Was it a particle, or a wave?

9
00:04:01,000 --> 00:04:26,000
SPEAKERA: That's the puzzle at the centre of the double-slit experiment.

10
00:04:31,000 --> 00:04:56,000
SPEAKERB: People argued for centuries about what light actually is.

11
00:05:01,000 --> 00:05:26,000
SPEAKERA: In his Treatise on Light, of sixteen ninety, Christiaan Huygens described light as a wave.

12
00:05:31,000 --> 00:05:56,000
SPEAKERB: And Isaac Newton's Opticks, from 1704, leaned toward particles.

13
00:06:01,000 --> 00:06:26,000
SPEAKERA: In later editions he asked, are not the rays of light very small bodies, emitted from shining substances?

14
00:06:31,000 --> 00:06:56,000
SPEAKERB: So tiny bodies fly out of anything that shines.

15
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:26,000
SPEAKERA: And Newton's reputation was so great that, through the eighteenth century, the particle view held a wide lead.

16
00:07:31,000 --> 00:07:56,000
SPEAKERB: So how would you even tell a wave from a stream of particles?

17
00:08:01,000 --> 00:08:26,000
SPEAKERA: Send particles through two slits, and you'd expect two bright bands, one behind each slit.

18
00:08:31,000 --> 00:08:56,000
SPEAKERA: But waves can do something particles can't. When two meet, they can add up, or cancel out.

19
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:26,000
SPEAKERB: Cancel out? Two waves can add up to nothing?

20
00:09:31,000 --> 00:09:56,000
SPEAKERA: Crest on crest makes a bigger wave; crest on trough makes nothing at all. That's interference.
